  "year": 2012,
  "authority": "Library of Congress / American Library Association, Persian Romanization Table (2012 version; earlier 2011, 1997)",
  "description": "Library romanization of Persian with Arabic-style vowels a i u, ā ī ū, ay aw, dots and other marks on the Arabic homophone letters (s̱ ṣ, ẕ z̤ ẓ, ṭ, ḥ), ʻ for ʿayn and ʼ for hamza, final silent h = -ah (khānah), ezafe -i / -yi / -ʼi, Arabic article al- without assimilation.",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.loc.gov/catdir/cpso/romanization/persian.pdf",
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Rule 2(a): silent و after خ is retained (khvāstan). Rule 7: tashdīd doubles; on و/ی after a long vowel it gives ūv/īy (qūvah, nashrīyāt).",
    "Rule 8: ezafe -i after a consonant, -yi after a vowel, -ʼi when marked with hamzah (ۀ); in personal names it is shown only when written (8d), which is what this scheme does (kasra at the end of a word, ۀ, هٔ).",
    "Rules 9-10: an affix or compound element written separately (ZWNJ, U+200C) is separated by a prime ʹ (Kāẓimʹzādah); compound personal names written with a space stay separate words (Ghulām ʻAlī), but a solid spelling (غلامعلی) cannot be split automatically.",
    "Rule 12(a): a prime separates letters that could be read as a digraph (marzʹhā): zʹh, sʹh, kʹh, gʹh; not applied to a word-final silent h.",
    "ة after a short vowel is -ah like a final silent h; after ā it is t (Note 4: ة used for ت).",
    "The ALA-LC macron/line below on ث (s̱) and ذ (ẕ) was lost in PDF extraction; values follow the Wikipedia comparison table."
  ],
  "alphabet": "ءآأؤئابپتثجچحخدذرزژسشصضطظعغفقکگلمنوهیةۀ",
